import{_ as t,o,c as a,a0 as n}from"./chunks/framework.9TNW7Bv-.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"One entry for every door, one document, permissions declared on the parts","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"OneEntry.md","filePath":"OneEntry.md"}'),s={name:"OneEntry.md"};function i(d,e,r,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="one-entry-for-every-door-one-document-permissions-declared-on-the-parts" tabindex="-1">One entry for every door, one document, permissions declared on the parts <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-entry-for-every-door-one-document-permissions-declared-on-the-parts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One entry for every door, one document, permissions declared on the parts&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The front end used to be two apps chosen by the hostname (<code>session/providerBoot.js</code> <code>frontendRole</code>): a namespace&#39;s own hosts (cleaker.me, www, a handle) got a bare <code>Namespace</code> landing, every other host (netget.site, localhost) got the <code>&lt;Cleaker&gt;</code> shell with netget&#39;s document. Same bundle, different sidebar: Monad existed on localhost and not on cleaker.me. A first fix mounted one shell but still composed TWO documents by door, so an administrator could not reach Domains from the namespace door.</p><h2 id="the-rule" tabindex="-1">The rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rule&quot;">​</a></h2><blockquote><p>The document declares what the app <strong>offers</strong>; the <strong>selected link</strong> and the permissions it grants decide what a person can <strong>use</strong>.</p></blockquote><ul><li><p><code>App.jsx</code> mounts <code>&lt;Cleaker&gt;</code> once, with ONE document (<code>NETGET_DOCUMENT</code>, <code>session/entryContext.js</code>). The address contributes <strong>context</strong> only: which namespace the monad or the gateway declared and where it is reached, and how the door presents itself (rail icons it leaves out, the node its position label points at). <code>frontendRole</code> and its test are unchanged; <code>local.host</code> is a different surface (HostSurface), not a composition of this app.</p></li><li><p>Each part declares what it needs, on the link <strong>and</strong> on the page behind its route:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>part</th><th><code>requires</code></th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monad, Dashboard, Apps, Terms, Privacy</td><td>nothing</td><td>public information; the shell dims what needs a layer that is off (<code>needs</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Domains</td><td><code>{ capability: &#39;gateway:admin&#39; }</code></td><td>this netget recognizes the identity signed in through the selected link as owner/administrator (a signed <code>/check-auth</code>), or a gateway in the list accepts the admin session stored for it</td></tr><tr><td>Logs</td><td><code>{ capability: &#39;gateway:logs&#39; }</code></td><td>the <strong>selected</strong> gateway is this netget, and it granted that identity the explicit scope <code>gateway:control:logs-read</code> (<code>lua/handlers/logs.lua</code> has no owner bypass)</td></tr></tbody></table></li><li><p>The shell (this.gui, <code>typedocs/Declarative-Requirements.md</code>) calls one resolver per capability with the <strong>selected link</strong>, the <strong>session through it</strong> and an app scope (<code>session/gatewayCapabilities.js</code>; the scope is &quot;which gateway is selected, and which admin sessions are stored&quot;, <code>gatewayScope</code>). Fail closed; results belong to (link, identity, scope); a late answer for another key is dropped. The GUI never learns what <code>gateway:admin</code> means, and the gateway still authorizes every request.</p></li><li><p>Why Domains does not follow the selected gateway: the gateway selector lives <strong>inside</strong> that screen. Hiding the screen because the selected gateway refuses you would leave no way back (to select this netget, or to paste a session). So Domains is the console (offered to whoever administers this netget or holds an accepted session for any listed gateway), and what it may do is decided inside it, per selected gateway and its own session, as before. Logs has no selector and does follow the selection.</p></li><li><p>Logs now sends the credentials of the <strong>selected gateway</strong> (<code>utils/gatewayFetch.js</code>, <code>session/useGatewayFetch.js</code>): this netget is asked with the signed proof of the identity signed in through the selected link (what nginx&#39;s <code>/logs</code> asks for); a gateway that was added, with its own admin session as a bearer token at its own origin. The shell&#39;s identity is never signed for another gateway and one gateway&#39;s token never goes to another.</p></li></ul><h2 id="what-was-verified" tabindex="-1">What was verified <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-was-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What was verified&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><code>tests/entry-composition.test.ts</code>, <code>tests/gateway-capabilities.test.ts</code>, <code>tests/gateway-fetch.test.ts</code>, <code>tests/gateway-standing.test.ts</code> (and, in the GUI, <code>tests/capabilities.test.ts</code> + <code>tests/integration/capabilities.browser.test.mjs</code>: real shell, real Chromium, two links, identity and scope changes, late answers).</p></li><li><p>Disposable gateway (its own OpenResty, backend and monad), real Chromium, identities registered and signed in through the real forms (30 conditions on one gateway, plus 11 with two links): signed in is not administrator (no Domains/Logs); owner without the logs-read grant gets Domains but not Logs; with the grant, both, and Logs sends <code>X-Me-Proof</code> and the gateway answers 200; selecting another gateway (no session for it) takes Logs away and keeps Domains, selecting this netget brings it back; <strong>an administrator reaches Domains and Logs from the namespace door</strong>; a different identity through the same door is not enabled by her grant; recognized-but-not-administrator is still neither; typing <code>/domains</code> does not render it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Two real links of the shell, together, in netget</strong> (real gateway and monad; link A = the root <code>localhost</code>, link B = the handle <code>bz.localhost</code>, each with its own session; 11 conditions): A (administrator) is offered Domains and Logs; selecting B with nobody signed in through it hides them and asks the gateway nothing; signing in through B keeps them hidden, and the gateway receives the real question, signed by B&#39;s identity, and does not recognize it; selecting A again finds its session still open and they return; B again hides them.</p></li><li><p>The recorrido scripts (rig, claims writer, the two journeys) are kept outside <code>/tmp</code>, in <code>~/.get/evidence/one-entry-capabilities-2026-10/</code>.</p></li></ul><h2 id="integration-candidate" tabindex="-1">Integration candidate <a class="header-anchor" href="#integration-candidate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Integration candidate&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Order: the GUI first (netget needs its new API), then netget. Nothing below is applied.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>branch</th><th>contents</th><th>version to decide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>this.gui</td><td><code>feat/declarative-capabilities</code> (on <code>main</code>, fast-forward)</td><td>declarative <code>requires</code>, <code>capabilities</code>/<code>capabilityScope</code>, <code>useGuiCapability</code>; additive, nothing existing changes</td><td>additive: a minor (4.3.0) by the CHANGELOG&#39;s own precedent</td></tr><tr><td>netget</td><td><code>feat/one-entry-composition</code> (on <code>main</code>, fast-forward)</td><td>one entry, one document, resolvers, Logs credentials, identity row on Domains</td><td>a minor (2.8.0), with <code>this.gui</code> raised to the GUI version that carries the API (<code>^4.3.0</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>Not in the candidate on purpose: the builder and the component library (they would consume this same contract), minting the admin session from the shell&#39;s identity, Logs for a gateway that was added.</li><li>Reaching cleaker.me needs a new release (netget front end + the GUI dist) and a VM activation: separate go.</li></ul><h2 id="known-not-changed-here" tabindex="-1">Known, not changed here <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-not-changed-here" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known, not changed here&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A grant given while a page is open is picked up when the key changes (sign in again, or select another gateway / store a session): the shell does not poll.</li><li>The Domains screen&#39;s write gate (<code>/add-domain</code> &amp; co.) accepts an admin SESSION with <code>gateway:write</code> (a bearer token), not the shell&#39;s signed proof. So the permission block now says both facts instead of contradicting the &quot;Unlocked&quot; chip: a row <strong>Esta identidad</strong> (what this netget answers to the signed <code>/check-auth</code>: owner / administrator / recognized / not recognized; <code>utils/identityStanding.js</code>) and, for an administrator with no session stored, what is missing to write. Letting the shell&#39;s identity mint that session (the existing <code>/admin-session</code> challenge, signed with the identity&#39;s keychain key) is the way to remove the extra step; it is not done here.</li><li>&quot;Conectar la primera puerta&quot; is enabled for a non-administrator on an empty list (the server refuses the write).</li><li>The <code>url</code> item (globe) used to show on cleaker.me; it is hidden now, as it already was on the gateway doors.</li><li>Logs for a gateway that was added are not offered: nginx&#39;s <code>/logs</code> accepts only this machine&#39;s own signed proof, not a bearer token.</li><li><code>eslint</code> reports four errors in code that was already there (App.jsx <code>wrapped</code> display-name, Logs <code>_log</code>, DoorRouter props, gatewayProbe <code>json</code>).</li></ul>',13)])])}const w=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,w as default};
